## Statement

Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $L$ be a nonempty smooth oriented link in the oriented $3$-sphere, and let $F_0,F_1$ be connected compact smooth oriented embedded surfaces with the same oriented boundary $L$. There is a finite sequence of such spanning surfaces from $F_0$ to $F_1$ in which each consecutive pair differs either by an ambient isotopy fixed on $L$ or by one embedded arc surgery or its reverse.

## Statement references

[Lickorish, Theorem 9.2, printed pp. 79–81](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4612-0691-0_9)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Full AC, a nonempty oriented link $L\subset S^3$, and connected compact oriented spanning surfaces $F_0,F_1$ with boundary $L$.

[A1] Full AC implies dependent choice and countable choice. [[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice]]

[F1] The one-variable cover of $S^3\setminus L$ is the connected cover classified by the kernel of the total-linking homomorphism, which sends every positive meridian to $1$. [[def-one-variable-alexander-module-of-an-oriented-link]]

[F2] After a perturbation fixed on a collar of $L$, the two compact cut surfaces meet transversely in finitely many curves; isotopy extension makes this a change of the embedded pair. [[thm-transversality-homotopy-theorem]], [[prop-relative-transversality-preserves-a-map-on-a-closed-good-region]], [[lem-isotopy-extension-for-a-compact-source-with-boundary]]

[F3] A compact smooth manifold with corners has a finite triangulation relative to its already triangulated boundary faces. [[thm-compact-smooth-manifolds-with-corners-admit-relative-finite-triangulations]]

[F4] The arc-surgery operation removes two disks and replaces them with the lateral annulus of an embedded product cylinder, leaving the boundary link fixed. [[def-seifert-surface-arc-surgery]]

## Proof

1.1 Cut the link exterior open along $F_0$ and stack copies of the cut exterior indexed by $\mathbb Z$, gluing the negative face of copy $n$ to the positive face of copy $n+1$. A loop changes its sheet by its signed intersection with $F_0$; each positive meridian intersects once positively, so by [F1] this is exactly the total-linking cover. Repeating the construction with $F_1$ gives the same cover, since both classify the same kernel. Lift the cut surfaces and choose compact lifts $\widetilde F_0,\widetilde F_1$. Their transverse intersection away from the common boundary collar is a compact one-manifold with finitely many components by [F2]. The deck translates of $\widetilde F_0$ are locally finite, so the compact $\widetilde F_1$ meets only finitely many of them. If an interior intersection exists, choose the largest integer $n$ for which $\widetilde F_1\cap t^n\widetilde F_0\ne\varnothing$. The component of the region on the negative side of $\widetilde F_1$ between $t^n\widetilde F_0$ and $t^{n+1}\widetilde F_0$ projects injectively to the exterior: a translate with exponent $>n$ misses that side, and one with exponent $<n$ misses the chosen fundamental slab by maximality. Its closure is therefore a compact complementary region $M$ whose boundary meets each $F_i$ only from one chosen side. If there is no interior intersection, take the closure of either product-collar region between their boundary collars. [A1, F1, F2, given, construct]

2.1 Triangulate this compact region relative to the two surface faces by [F3], making their intersection and the boundary collar subcomplexes. In the second barycentric subdivision take a closed regular neighborhood $A$ of the part of $\partial M$ on the chosen side of $F_0$, together with every edge of the triangulation, and put $B=\overline{M\setminus A}$. Replace the portion of $F_0$ on $\partial M$ by the other boundary of $A$; replacing the collar of that face is an isotopy, and passing each edge neighborhood changes the surface by replacing two disks by the lateral annulus of a product cylinder, which is exactly [F4]. The finite 1-skeleton means this uses finitely many moves. Perform the corresponding replacement for $F_1$ using $B$. Since $M$ lies on only the selected side of each surface, these replacements do not introduce intersections outside $M$. Within $M$, the new intersection consists only of the old intersection outside the chosen face plus the boundary of the regular neighborhood; pushing the first replacement a small distance into its selected collar removes the latter boundary. Thus the number of interior intersection components strictly decreases whenever it was positive. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, construct]

3.1 The number of interior intersection components is a nonnegative integer, so repeating step 2.1 finitely many times leaves the surfaces disjoint away from the fixed boundary collar. Apply the same neighborhood construction once more in the closure of the region between them: all of one surface face is now a subcomplex disjoint from the other, so its regular neighborhood boundary is a parallel copy of the other face. The replacement is an ambient isotopy across the product collar, fixed on $L$. Reversing the finite moves made to $F_1$ yields the required finite sequence from $F_0$ to $F_1$. [F2, F3, step 2.1, construct] ∎
